  "notes": "Go import resolution is package-granular: an import names a directory, not a file, so a resolved import fans out to every non-test file of the package directory with symbol `*` (the whole package really is visible to the importer). The module path is deduced from the corpus (the extractor is not handed `go.mod`, and will not open a file it was not given), then stripped to find the directory. `*_test.go` files are excluded from the package index — `shape_test.go` declares a *different* package (`shape_test`) in the same directory, which is the dir-to-package mapping trap. The `package` clause is a file's identity, not a definition. Export = first rune is an upper-case letter, which is a name property, not a declaration marker. Two files with `//go:build` constraints both declare `metrics.Init`. Edges are drawn per file of the imported package and every one of them is labelled package-granular: the language's dependency is `main -> shape`, and the file-level rendering is the store's granularity, not the language's (e3.1b, s1 finding 4). The module path is deduced from the corpus (the extractor is not handed `go.mod` and will not open it); an import that fits neither the deduced module nor a corpus-relative path comes back unresolved.",
